90601aa25" :question/math-academy-id "q-18372" :question/problem "Let $c=⟨-2,0,0⟩$ and $d=⟨4,-1,0⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $c$ onto $d.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c & =\\frac{c\\cdot d}{|\\,d\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{d}_{x}^{2}+{d}_{y}^{2}+{d}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\cdot 4+0\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{17}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-18372/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91691448-c59a-56dc-a775-d4e9c3297036"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-18372"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-19139/selection/1f1270eb9d606883b8f0b29d4b3c52aaeb8c93d502b5ca4185fe7672b127e08e" :answer/id #uuid "3f11c720-e046-523b-8dc7-c52f825eba0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{19}{\\sqrt{26}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-19139/selection/eee32b7cce886904f5011d82c2f95af1485a18b3ba86fce38e3c69db5f4fbecb" :answer/id #uuid "08f2859a-5d24-534f-82e7-b3fbf2ac9ede" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{19}{\\sqrt{14}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-19139/selection/16288a6920bb6233cbd613977c2719486d811d70d6225b7eec30abd55fa72e79" :answer/id #uuid "6c699610-261c-5695-b196-3999bf781137" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{26}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-19139/selection/7e807d99f0c5e06334600d9b8a1871252aba4af8e14475e745e2f94f171feaf2" :answer/id #uuid "ba533aff-71f4-54f1-a488-8e6bd6e66a13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{14}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-19139/selection/62f950483e517c09df6d9d87241e581869b8e6e12cc62c9a403cf4cf683db929" :answer/id #uuid "5cd6b170-da5f-508f-a233-6372ca4cf73d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{26}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-19139/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c23d68cf-4f67-573a-91f0-15f4e696233a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-19139/selection/62f950483e517c09df6d9d87241e581869b8e6e12cc62c9a403cf4cf683db929" :answer-field/choices ["q-19139/selection/1f1270eb9d606883b8f0b29d4b3c52aaeb8c93d502b5ca4185fe7672b127e08e" "q-19139/selection/eee32b7cce886904f5011d82c2f95af1485a18b3ba86fce38e3c69db5f4fbecb" "q-19139/selection/16288a6920bb6233cbd613977c2719486d811d70d6225b7eec30abd55fa72e79" "q-19139/selection/7e807d99f0c5e06334600d9b8a1871252aba4af8e14475e745e2f94f171feaf2" "q-19139/selection/62f950483e517c09df6d9d87241e581869b8e6e12cc62c9a403cf4cf683db929"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-19139" :question/id #uuid "26907092-d115-5133-80f7-1ed5a1bdc6ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-19139" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨3,2,1⟩$ and $b=⟨3,4,1⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{26}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-19139/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91691448-c59a-56dc-a775-d4e9c3297036"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-19139"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-236734"] :question/problem "Let $p=⟨-1,1,2⟩$ and $q=⟨4,x,1⟩.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 4+1\\cdot x+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-236735"] :question/problem "Let $p=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $q=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =6\\cdot x+1\\cdot 4+4\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =6x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 6x-12 & =0 \\\\ 6x & =12 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-319254/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "9598539e-b54f-5c35-85a9-860683f593e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-319254/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7c5e5c8a-f0ab-52e4-a5aa-56854d500dd5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-319254/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-319254/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-319254" :question/id #uuid "1b038fbb-5dcb-597f-a35d-5322f573b512" :question/math-academy-id "q-319254" :question/problem "Let $p=3i+2j-k$ and $q=-2i+xj.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-6+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ -6+2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-319254/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a51e317c-f6b3-5704-afb4-bdfc79f1176f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-319254"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
